## Frontend Development (HTMX + Modern Web)

### HTMX Usage

- Use `hx-get`, `hx-post`, and other HTMX attributes to define server requests directly in HTML
- Structure server responses to return only necessary HTML snippets for updates
- Favor declarative attributes over JavaScript event handlers
- Leverage `hx-trigger` to customize event handling:
  - `hx-trigger="every 5s"` for auto-refresh in monitoring panels
  - `hx-trigger="load"` for initial data loading
  - `hx-trigger="change"` for filter updates
- Utilize `hx-target` to specify where response content should be injected
- Use `hx-swap` appropriately:
  - `innerHTML` for content updates
  - `outerHTML` for component replacement
  - `afterbegin`/`beforeend` for list additions

### Real-Time Updates

- Implement auto-refresh for monitoring panels:tml
  <div hx-get="/api/viagem/{{.ID}}/status" 
       hx-trigger="every 5s" 
       hx-target="#trip-status"
       hx-swap="innerHTML">
  - Provide manual refresh buttons with `hx-get` and `hx-trigger="click"`
- Show loading states with `hx-indicator` attribute
- Use `hx-push-url` to update browser URL without full page refresh

### Error Handling and Validation

- Implement **server-side validation** for all inputs
- Return appropriate HTTP status codes (4xx for client errors, 5xx for server errors)
- Display user-friendly error messages using HTMX:
  <div hx-target="#error-message" hx-swap="innerHTML">
    <!-- Error content -->
  </div>
  - Use `hx-confirm` for critical actions (trip dispatch, cancellations)

### Performance Optimization

- Minimize server response sizes by returning only essential HTML
- Implement caching strategies for frequently requested endpoints:
  - Geofence definitions
  - Vehicle lists
  - Farm/factory data
- Optimize HTML rendering by precompiling reusable fragments
- Use `hx-boost="true"` for progressive enhancement

